# What Vercel does NOT need as build input.
#
# `buildCommand` is `npm run build`, which is FOUR steps —
#   tsc --noEmit && tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.build.json && vite build && verify-dist.mjs
# — so the upload has to carry everything those four read, and nothing else. Measured 2026-08-26:
# excluding the entries below drops ~169 MB from every deploy.
#
# 🔴 EVERY DIRECTORY PATTERN IS ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, AND THAT IS NOT COSMETIC.
#
# `.vercelignore` uses gitignore syntax, in which a pattern with **no leading slash matches at ANY
# DEPTH**. So a bare `assets/` excludes `public/assets/` as well as `assets/` — and
# `public/assets/` is 48 of the 60 files under `public/`: every sprite sheet, tile set, parallax
# layer, portrait and sound the game loads.
#
# **This is not hypothetical. It is what the first deploy did**, on 2026-08-27:
#
#     Downloading 740 deployment files...
#     tests/unit/audio-catalog.test.ts(20,21): error TS2307:
#       Cannot find module '../../public/assets/index.json'
#     Error: Command "npm run build" exited with 1
#
# Every local gate was green. The build fails loudly on Vercel only because `tsconfig.json`
# includes `tests/`, and the tests import the catalog — had they not, this would have deployed a
# game with no art at all. That is the whole reason criterion 10.6's deploy half was recorded as
# OWED rather than assumed: the local production substrate cannot see what was never uploaded.
#
# Verified with git's own matcher rather than from memory:
#
#     bare `assets/`      hides public/assets/index.json: YES
#     anchored `/assets/` hides public/assets/index.json: no
#     anchored `/assets/` still hides assets/x.png:       YES
#
# `tests/unit/vercelignore.test.ts` fails on any unanchored directory pattern.
#
# 🔴 WHAT MUST NOT BE ADDED HERE, and why each would break the build rather than slow it:
#
#   tests/                 `tsconfig.json`'s include list names it. 3.8 MB.
#   playwright.config.ts   named individually in that same include list.
#   vercel.json            read by BOTH typecheck programs and by tools/gen/vercelHeaders.mjs.
#   public/                53 MB, and it IS the game: `vite build` copies it verbatim into
#                          dist/, and `verify-dist.mjs` asserts every .tmj and audio file
#                          arrived byte for byte.
#   tools/                 verify-dist.mjs, the dev-seam gate, vercelHeaders.mjs and the
#                          hand-written *.d.mts / node-shims.d.mts all live there, and the
#                          build program typechecks the .mjs bodies as well as the declarations.
#
# ⚠️ **The two `playwright.config.ts` lines above used to justify themselves with a claim that is
# FALSE**: *"tsc errors 'File not found' on a missing named include, not on an empty glob."* It does
# not. Measured twice on 2026-08-26 — a named include that matches nothing is silently ignored, and
# even removing `tests` from the include list typechecks clean; tsc errors only when EVERY pattern
# in the list matches nothing. So excluding these would NOT break the build loudly. It would shrink
# what the typecheck covers, silently, on the build box only — which is worse than the failure the
# original note promised, and is why `tests/unit/build-program.test.ts` now pins the include list.
#
# Adding any of those would produce a build failure on Vercel and a green build locally,
# which is the worst shape a config error can take.

# 73 MB — prose and captured evidence. No build step reads docs/.
/docs/

# 96 MB — Phase 0 style probes and reference art. Superseded by public/assets/; kept in the
# repo for provenance, never read by the build.
/assets/

# Local Playwright output. Regenerated per run, meaningless on a build box.
/test-results/
/playwright-report/

# The build's own output. Uploading it would ship a stale bundle as the input to building a
# fresh one.
/dist/

# Raw model output. Already gitignored, so it is absent from a clone anyway — listed so the
# intent survives if that ever changes.
/_generated/

# 🔴 THE LINES BELOW ARE NOT OPTIONAL, AND THE REASON IS A DOCUMENTED VERCEL BEHAVIOUR.
#
# Vercel's own documentation (docs/deployments/vercel-ignore, read 2026-08-26): *"By default, ALL
# files within your project are uploaded to Vercel unless explicitly excluded in .vercelignore."*
# `.gitignore` is **not consulted** — it appears in Vercel's default-exclusion list, which means the
# FILE is not uploaded, not that its RULES are applied. The default exclusions are a fixed list
# (`.git`, `node_modules`, `.env.local`, `.next`, `.vercel`, a handful more) and nothing else.
#
# So an untracked, gitignored directory in the working tree is uploaded in full. Measured here:
# `.claude/` is **1.4 GB** on this machine — worktrees, plans and caches, 215 tracked files and a
# very large amount of local state — and every byte of it would have gone up with every deploy.
# Neither directory is read by `npm run build`.
/.claude/
/.agents/

# Belt and braces: both are on Vercel's default-exclusion list, but that list is a property of the
# platform rather than of this repository, and `.gitignore` does NOT back it up here.
/node_modules/
/.git/
